  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Collecting Asian Art shows clear early use through 1,733 OAPEN downloads and open-access availability, with additional support from KU Leuven and FWF - Der Wissenschaftsfonds. The book is aligned with SDG-related concerns around sustainable cities and communities, while its evidence base is still developing: OpenAlex currently records no citations.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Collecting Asian Art is a 2024 scholarly book from Leuven University Press that examines museum collections of Asian art in Central Europe, shifting attention away from the better-known Western European and North American settings. Its framing spans the late nineteenth century through the post-World War II period, and the subject matter connects strongly with history, politics, art history, East Asia, and the cultural conditions that shaped collecting practices across changing regimes and transnational networks.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest available impact signal is use: the book has been downloaded 1,733 times via OAPEN, indicating sustained reader interest. It is also openly accessible through a hybrid open-access model, which likely supports that reach. At the same time, the citation record is still at zero in OpenAlex, so the scholarly uptake visible in citation data has not yet emerged in the sources currently indexed.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The record also shows structured institutional backing, with funding credited to KU Leuven and FWF - Der Wissenschaftsfonds. That support sits alongside the book\u2019s cross-institutional authorship and its connection to a Prague conference on collecting Asian art, suggesting a project embedded in international scholarly exchange rather than a narrowly local publication pathway.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Beyond usage and publication support, the book has an SDG association with Sustainable cities and communities, which is consistent with its focus on museums, cultural heritage, and the civic life of collections. The evidence for public discourse is limited in the current record, so the clearest story here is one of open availability, early reader uptake, and disciplinary relevance, with longer-term citation impact still to be established.</p>",
